{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"id\":\"R-4:121\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-4:135\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"The command to uphold justice even against personal or family interest gives a general constraint for the choice between تُعَذِّبَ and تَتَّخِذَ فِيهِمْ حُسْنًا.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"4:135\",\"ar\":\"كُونُوا۟ قَوَّٰمِينَ بِٱلْقِسْطِ شُهَدَآءَ لِلَّهِ وَلَوْ عَلَىٰٓ أَنفُسِكُمْ أَوِ ٱلْوَٰلِدَيْنِ وَٱلْأَقْرَبِينَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus presents authority to choose how to treat قَوْمًا.\",\"limits\":\"The focus gives no sign of kinship or personal bias, so this verse does not decide which option applies.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"low\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-4:173\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The focus's إِمَّآ...وَإِمَّآ branches are resolved in 18:87–88, then echoed by 4:173's paired outcomes for believers and the arrogant. This frames Dhu al-Qarnayn's local decision alongside a larger divine judgment without equating their authority.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"18:86\",\"ar\":\"إِمَّآ أَن تُعَذِّبَ وَإِمَّآ أَن تَتَّخِذَ فِيهِمْ حُسْنًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"18:87\",\"ar\":\"أَمَّا مَن ظَلَمَ فَسَوْفَ نُعَذِّبُهُۥ\"},{\"ref\":\"18:88\",\"ar\":\"وَأَمَّا مَنْ ءَامَنَ وَعَمِلَ صَٰلِحًۭا فَلَهُۥ جَزَآءً ٱلْحُسْنَىٰ\"},{\"ref\":\"4:173\",\"ar\":\"فَأَمَّا ٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوا۟ وَعَمِلُوا۟ ٱلصَّٰلِحَٰتِ فَيُوَفِّيهِمْ أُجُورَهُمْ ... وَأَمَّا ٱلَّذِينَ ٱسْتَنكَفُوا۟ وَٱسْتَكْبَرُوا۟ فَيُعَذِّبُهُمْ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus uses paired alternatives, and its next two ayat divide people by wrongdoing and faith.\",\"limits\":\"4:173 describes God's judgment and uses different criteria; it does not identify Dhu al-Qarnayn's authority with God's.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-5:8\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The command not to let hostility toward a people prevent justice supplies an ethical test for the focus's choice between punishment and good treatment of قَوْمًا.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"5:8\",\"ar\":\"وَلَا يَجْرِمَنَّكُمْ شَنَـَٔانُ قَوْمٍ عَلَىٰٓ أَلَّا تَعْدِلُوا۟ ۚ ٱعْدِلُوا۟ هُوَ أَقْرَبُ لِلتَّقْوَىٰ\"},{\"ref\":\"18:86\",\"ar\":\"وَوَجَدَ عِندَهَا قَوْمًۭا ۗ قُلْنَا يَٰذَا ٱلْقَرْنَيْنِ إِمَّآ أَن تُعَذِّبَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus places a people before an authority empowered to choose their treatment.\",\"limits\":\"The focus does not say the people are hostile or that punishment would be motivated by hostility.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-5:45\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"The verse pairs injury-for-injury judgment with voluntary remission, offering a model in which consequence and leniency coexist; the focus's paired options can be heard beside that model.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"5:45\",\"ar\":\"وَٱلْجُرُوحَ قِصَاصٌۭ ۚ فَمَن تَصَدَّقَ بِهِۦ فَهُوَ كَفَّارَةٌۭ لَّهُۥ\"},{\"ref\":\"18:86\",\"ar\":\"إِمَّآ أَن تُعَذِّبَ وَإِمَّآ أَن تَتَّخِذَ فِيهِمْ حُسْنًۭا\"}],\"activation\":\"Both passages place punitive action beside a less punitive possibility.\",\"limits\":\"The focus does not name pardon, and 5:45 concerns retribution for injuries in a different legal setting.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-5:67\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"The Messenger is told to deliver revelation, while Dhu al-Qarnayn is addressed with discretion over a people's treatment; this may distinguish conveying a message from exercising delegated rule.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"5:67\",\"ar\":\"يَٰٓأَيُّهَا ٱلرَّسُولُ بَلِّغْ مَآ أُنزِلَ إِلَيْكَ مِن رَّبِّكَ\"},{\"ref\":\"18:86\",\"ar\":\"قُلْنَا يَٰذَا ٱلْقَرْنَيْنِ إِمَّآ أَن تُعَذِّبَ وَإِمَّآ أَن تَتَّخِذَ فِيهِمْ حُسْنًۭا\"}],\"missing\":\"The focus does not identify Dhu al-Qarnayn as a Messenger or compare his role with one; an explicit role contrast elsewhere in the surah or another ayah could establish the boundary.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-5:84\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-5:99\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"same-as:R-5:67\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-5:104\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"وَجَدْنَا عَلَيْهَآ describes people finding their forefathers committed to a practice, whereas the focus uses وَجَدَهَا and وَجَدَ for a witnessed sunset and encountered people. The contrast could distinguish inherited claims from immediate encounter.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"5:104\",\"ar\":\"قَالُوا۟ حَسْبُنَا مَا وَجَدْنَا عَلَيْهِ ءَابَآءَنَآ\"},{\"ref\":\"18:86\",\"ar\":\"وَجَدَهَا تَغْرُبُ فِى عَيْنٍ حَمِئَةٍۢ وَوَجَدَ عِندَهَا قَوْمًۭا\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus repeats the root و ج د for what Dhu al-Qarnayn observes and encounters.\",\"missing\":\"Nothing in the focus disputes inherited authority; a wider passage connecting observation with claims based on precedent would be needed to establish that contrast as part of its reading.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-6:78\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Ibrahim watches the sun set and then rejects what his people associate with it; this Quranic sunset scene makes the focus's وَجَدَهَا تَغْرُبُ perceptual framing salient, without turning Dhu al-Qarnayn's report into a theological argument.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"6:78\",\"ar\":\"فَلَمَّا رَءَا ٱلشَّمْسَ بَازِغَةًۭ قَالَ هَٰذَا رَبِّى هَٰذَآ أَكْبَرُ ۖ فَلَمَّآ أَفَلَتْ قَالَ يَٰقَوْمِ إِنِّى بَرِىٓءٌۭ مِّمَّا تُشْرِكُونَ\"},{\"ref\":\"18:86\",\"ar\":\"وَجَدَهَا تَغْرُبُ فِى عَيْنٍ حَمِئَةٍۢ\"}],\"activation\":\"Both scenes make the sun's setting an observed event.\",\"limits\":\"Ibrahim's scene argues against associating partners with God; the focus proceeds to an encounter and a governance choice.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-6:152\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The حسن root in the rule protecting an orphan's wealth gives the focus's حُسْنًا فِيهِمْ an ethical resonance of acting for others' welfare, beyond simply selecting a pleasant outcome.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"6:152\",\"ar\":\"وَلَا تَقْرَبُوا۟ مَالَ ٱلْيَتِيمِ إِلَّا بِٱلَّتِى هِىَ أَحْسَنُ حَتَّىٰ يَبْلُغَ أَشُدَّهُۥ\"},{\"ref\":\"18:86\",\"ar\":\"تَتَّخِذَ فِيهِمْ حُسْنًۭا\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus names goodness as the alternative treatment offered to a people.\",\"limits\":\"6:152 concerns an orphan's property and uses the comparative أَحْسَنُ; it does not define حُسْنًا here as guardianship.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-7:28\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"The focus narrates divine speech authorizing a ruler's choice; 7:28 depicts people falsely claiming that God commanded their practice after invoking what they found their forefathers doing. The contrast could distinguish narrated authorization from human claims of it.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"7:28\",\"ar\":\"وَجَدْنَا عَلَيْهَآ ءَابَآءَنَا وَٱللَّهُ أَمَرَنَا بِهَا\"},{\"ref\":\"18:86\",\"ar\":\"قُلْنَا يَٰذَا ٱلْقَرْنَيْنِ إِمَّآ أَن تُعَذِّبَ وَإِمَّآ أَن تَتَّخِذَ فِيهِمْ حُسْنًۭا\"}],\"activation\":\"Both passages connect وَجَدَ language with a claim or instruction about conduct.\",\"missing\":\"The focus gives no sign of disputed authorization or ancestral practice; a surrounding passage that places Dhu al-Qarnayn's instruction against such claims could support the comparison.\",\"support\":\"weak\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-7:115\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The contest uses the same paired frame, إِمَّآ أَن ... وَإِمَّآ أَن, as the focus. The form packages two alternative actions, while the actors and stakes differ sharply.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"7:115\",\"ar\":\"إِمَّآ أَن تُلْقِىَ وَإِمَّآ أَن نَّكُونَ نَحْنُ ٱلْمُلْقِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"18:86\",\"ar\":\"إِمَّآ أَن تُعَذِّبَ وَإِمَّآ أَن تَتَّخِذَ فِيهِمْ حُسْنًۭا\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus sets out two options with the exact same construction.\",\"limits\":\"In 7:115 the alternatives concern who casts first in a contest, not how a ruler treats a people.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-7:135\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-8:33\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"God's refraining from punishing a people while the Messenger is among them or they seek forgiveness may qualify a broad account of punishment; the focus, however, puts a local choice before Dhu al-Qarnayn.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"8:33\",\"ar\":\"وَمَا كَانَ ٱللَّهُ لِيُعَذِّبَهُمْ وَأَنتَ فِيهِمْ ۚ وَمَا كَانَ ٱللَّهُ مُعَذِّبَهُمْ وَهُمْ يَسْتَغْفِرُونَ\"},{\"ref\":\"18:86\",\"ar\":\"إِمَّآ أَن تُعَذِّبَ وَإِمَّآ أَن تَتَّخِذَ فِيهِمْ حُسْنًۭا\"}],\"activation\":\"Both passages put punishment in view for a people.\",\"missing\":\"The focus says nothing about a Messenger being present or the people seeking forgiveness; evidence that this divine restraint applies to Dhu al-Qarnayn's delegated decision would be needed.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-8:61\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"The command to accept peace when an opposing group inclines toward it offers a condition for choosing a nonviolent response alongside the focus's two options.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"8:61\",\"ar\":\"وَإِن جَنَحُوا۟ لِلسَّلْمِ فَٱجْنَحْ لَهَا\"},{\"ref\":\"18:86\",\"ar\":\"إِمَّآ أَن تُعَذِّبَ وَإِمَّآ أَن تَتَّخِذَ فِيهِمْ حُسْنًۭا\"}],\"activation\":\"Dhu al-Qarnayn is given a choice about treatment after encountering a people.\",\"missing\":\"The focus does not describe conflict or a peace offer; evidence that the people are negotiating peace would make this a specific guide to the choice.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-9:123\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"The nearby group in the focus is identified by location, whereas 9:123 commands fighting those nearby who are explicitly among the disbelievers. This contrast can test whether proximity alone bears on treatment.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"9:123\",\"ar\":\"قَٰتِلُوا۟ ٱلَّذِينَ يَلُونَكُم مِّنَ ٱلْكُفَّارِ وَلْيَجِدُوا۟ فِيكُمْ غِلْظَةًۭ\"},{\"ref\":\"18:86\",\"ar\":\"وَوَجَدَ عِندَهَا قَوْمًۭا ۗ قُلْنَا يَٰذَا ٱلْقَرْنَيْنِ إِمَّآ أَن تُعَذِّبَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus places a people near the sunset locus before introducing punitive authority.\",\"missing\":\"The focus does not call these people disbelievers or enemies; evidence of their status or conduct would be needed to apply 9:123 to them.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-11:3\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-11:40\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"Both passages use حَتَّىٰٓ إِذَا before an endpoint and then introduce divine speech with قُلْنَا; the repeated narrative frame marks arrival as a transition into a commanded action.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"11:40\",\"ar\":\"حَتَّىٰٓ إِذَا جَآءَ أَمْرُنَا وَفَارَ ٱلتَّنُّورُ قُلْنَا ٱحْمِلْ فِيهَا\"},{\"ref\":\"18:86\",\"ar\":\"حَتَّىٰٓ إِذَا بَلَغَ مَغْرِبَ ٱلشَّمْسِ ... قُلْنَا يَٰذَا ٱلْقَرْنَيْنِ\"}],\"activation\":\"In both, an endpoint clause leads into a divine utterance.\",\"limits\":\"11:40 is a flood-rescue command, not a grant of discretion about a newly encountered people.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-12:22\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"Yusuf's بلوغ أَشُدَّهُ is followed by judgment and knowledge, while Dhu al-Qarnayn's reaching a place is followed by a decision about his power. The shared بلغ threshold can associate arrival with a new phase of action.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"12:22\",\"ar\":\"وَلَمَّا بَلَغَ أَشُدَّهُۥٓ ءَاتَيْنَٰهُ حُكْمًۭا وَعِلْمًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"18:86\",\"ar\":\"إِذَا بَلَغَ مَغْرِبَ ٱلشَّمْسِ\"}],\"activation\":\"Both passages use بلغ at a threshold before a consequential next step.\",\"limits\":\"One threshold is maturity and the other is geographic; the shared verb does not make their grants of authority equivalent.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-12:54\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"The king calls Yusuf secure and trustworthy in his court; the focus places Dhu al-Qarnayn under divine address after describing his empowerment. Together they suggest authority as a trust placed in an agent.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"12:54\",\"ar\":\"قَالَ إِنَّكَ ٱلْيَوْمَ لَدَيْنَا مَكِينٌ أَمِينٌۭ\"},{\"ref\":\"18:86\",\"ar\":\"قُلْنَا يَٰذَا ٱلْقَرْنَيْنِ إِمَّآ أَن تُعَذِّبَ\"}],\"activation\":\"Both scenes move an empowered figure toward a consequential exercise of authority.\",\"missing\":\"The focus does not characterize Dhu al-Qarnayn as أمين or describe his appointment in courtly terms; shared wording or an explicit trust motif could establish a closer link.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-12:55\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"Yusuf offers preservation and knowledge as grounds for managing the land's storehouses, a stewardship model relevant in broad terms to Dhu al-Qarnayn's use of power.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"12:55\",\"ar\":\"إِنِّى حَفِيظٌ عَلِيمٌۭ\"},{\"ref\":\"18:86\",\"ar\":\"إِمَّآ أَن تُعَذِّبَ وَإِمَّآ أَن تَتَّخِذَ فِيهِمْ حُسْنًۭا\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus puts the use of authority over people into view.\",\"limits\":\"The focus says nothing about managing resources or what qualifications Dhu al-Qarnayn has.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"low\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-12:65\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-12:75\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"In Yusuf's episode, finding someone with the missing cup is made the stated basis for penalty; in the focus, finding a people near the spring is followed by a choice, not a finding of guilt. The distinction becomes explicit when 18:87 directs punishment toward مَن ظَلَمَ.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"12:75\",\"ar\":\"جَزَٰٓؤُهُۥ مَن وُجِدَ فِى رَحْلِهِۦ فَهُوَ جَزَٰؤُهُۥ\"},{\"ref\":\"18:86\",\"ar\":\"وَوَجَدَ عِندَهَا قَوْمًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"18:87\",\"ar\":\"أَمَّا مَن ظَلَمَ فَسَوْفَ نُعَذِّبُهُۥ\"}],\"activation\":\"Both passages use finding language before a question of consequence, but identify different objects and grounds.\",\"limits\":\"12:75 reports a distinct royal legal arrangement; it does not prescribe the focus's decision.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-12:79\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Yusuf refuses to seize anyone except the person found with the property, calling broader seizure unjust. Beside the focus's encounter with an entire قَوْمًا, this stresses that proximity or collective presence does not establish each person's guilt; 18:87's مَن ظَلَمَ specifies the punitive target.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"12:79\",\"ar\":\"أَن نَّأْخُذَ إِلَّا مَن وَجَدْنَا مَتَٰعَنَا عِندَهُۥٓ إِنَّآ إِذًۭا لَّظَٰلِمُونَ\"},{\"ref\":\"18:86\",\"ar\":\"وَوَجَدَ عِندَهَا قَوْمًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"18:87\",\"ar\":\"أَمَّا مَن ظَلَمَ فَسَوْفَ نُعَذِّبُهُۥ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus moves from finding a collective nearby to a decision about their treatment.\",\"limits\":\"Yusuf's case concerns possession of identifiable property; the focus does not name a comparable offense or evidence.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-12:94\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"Yusuf's father says he finds Yusuf's scent; this confirms that وَجَدَ can describe sensory apprehension, complementing the focus's finding of the sun in a setting state.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"12:94\",\"ar\":\"إِنِّى لَأَجِدُ رِيحَ يُوسُفَ\"},{\"ref\":\"18:86\",\"ar\":\"وَجَدَهَا تَغْرُبُ فِى عَيْنٍ حَمِئَةٍۢ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus uses وَجَدَهَا with a clause describing what the sun is doing.\",\"limits\":\"The scent scene does not alter the focus's location or ethical choice.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"low\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-13:14\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-15:26\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The root ح م ء links the focus's حَمِئَةٍ spring to the primordial material حَمَإٍ in the creation account. The landscape at the sun's setting can therefore carry an echo of creation's mud, though the focus itself remains a description of a place.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"18:86\",\"ar\":\"فِى عَيْنٍ حَمِئَةٍۢ\"},{\"ref\":\"15:26\",\"ar\":\"وَلَقَدْ خَلَقْنَا ٱلْإِنسَٰنَ مِن صَلْصَٰلٍۢ مِّنْ حَمَإٍۢ مَّسْنُونٍۢ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus describes a muddy spring and the other ayah names mud as material in human creation.\",\"limits\":\"The adjective describing the spring is not the creation noun, and neither passage says the spring represents creation.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-15:28\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"The creation verse specifies that بشر is made from the same حَمَإٍ named in 15:26, so the muddy setting beside which Dhu al-Qarnayn finds people could be heard beside a shared human origin.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"15:28\",\"ar\":\"إِنِّى خَٰلِقٌۢ بَشَرًۭا مِّن صَلْصَٰلٍۢ مِّنْ حَمَإٍۢ مَّسْنُونٍۢ\"},{\"ref\":\"18:86\",\"ar\":\"فِى عَيْنٍ حَمِئَةٍۢ وَوَجَدَ عِندَهَا قَوْمًۭا\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus joins a muddy locus to the presence of a human collective.\",\"missing\":\"The focus does not say the people come from the spring or draw a lesson from their material origin; a further textual cue linking the place to human creation would be needed.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-15:33\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"Iblis invokes the human's creation from حَمَإٍ as a reason not to bow to بشر. Beside the focus's choice of how to treat a people, this offers a possible contrast between contempt for human origin and responsibility toward humans.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"15:33\",\"ar\":\"قَالَ لَمْ أَكُن لِّأَسْجُدَ لِبَشَرٍ خَلَقْتَهُۥ مِن صَلْصَٰلٍۢ مِّنْ حَمَإٍۢ مَّسْنُونٍۢ\"},{\"ref\":\"18:86\",\"ar\":\"وَجَدَ عِندَهَا قَوْمًۭا ... إِمَّآ أَن تُعَذِّبَ وَإِمَّآ أَن تَتَّخِذَ فِيهِمْ حُسْنًۭا\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus places a decision about human treatment beside a muddy spring; 15:33 gives mud a role in an argument about human worth.\",\"missing\":\"The focus gives no sign that Dhu al-Qarnayn's choice concerns human origins or Iblis's refusal; an intervening passage linking these themes could ground the contrast.\",\"support\":\"weak\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-16:35\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"The idolaters claim that God's will excuses their worship, while the focus narrates an actual divine address giving Dhu al-Qarnayn options. This could contrast claimed divine authorization with an instruction explicitly attributed to God.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"16:35\",\"ar\":\"لَوْ شَآءَ ٱللَّهُ مَا عَبَدْنَا مِن دُونِهِۦ مِن شَىْءٍۢ ... فَهَلْ عَلَى ٱلرُّسُلِ إِلَّا ٱلْبَلَٰغُ ٱلْمُبِينُ\"},{\"ref\":\"18:86\",\"ar\":\"قُلْنَا يَٰذَا ٱلْقَرْنَيْنِ إِمَّآ أَن تُعَذِّبَ وَإِمَّآ أَن تَتَّخِذَ فِيهِمْ حُسْنًۭا\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus explicitly attributes the decision frame to divine speech.\",\"missing\":\"The focus does not discuss fatalism, idolatry, or a contested claim about God's command; a wider link about agency or authorization would be needed.\",\"support\":\"weak\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-16:67\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The same verb root اتخذ and حسن pairing appears in a verse where people make good provision from fruit. This gives تَتَّخِذَ فِيهِمْ حُسْنًا a Quranic precedent for taking as producing or instituting a positive result, while the constructions differ.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"16:67\",\"ar\":\"تَتَّخِذُونَ مِنْهُ سَكَرًۭا وَرِزْقًا حَسَنًا\"},{\"ref\":\"18:86\",\"ar\":\"تَتَّخِذَ فِيهِمْ حُسْنًۭا\"}],\"activation\":\"Both phrases pair a form of اتخذ with حسن language.\",\"limits\":\"In 16:67 حسنًا is an adjective of رزق, while in the focus حُسْنًا is a noun object; the verse does not define the focus's social meaning.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-16:90\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The command to practice العدل and الإحسان gives a compact ethical frame for the focus's choice between تُعَذِّبَ and حُسْنًا فِيهِمْ: justice can govern punitive response, while benevolence names the constructive branch.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"16:90\",\"ar\":\"إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ يَأْمُرُ بِٱلْعَدْلِ وَٱلْإِحْسَٰنِ\"},{\"ref\":\"18:86\",\"ar\":\"إِمَّآ أَن تُعَذِّبَ وَإِمَّآ أَن تَتَّخِذَ فِيهِمْ حُسْنًۭا\"}],\"activation\":\"Both ayat frame conduct toward others through justice and good treatment.\",\"limits\":\"The focus does not explicitly identify punishment with عدل or حُسْنًا with إحسان; the correspondence is ethical rather than a direct lexical equation.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-17:15\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The rule that no bearer carries another's burden offers a limit on collective blame. The focus first names a قَوْمًا, while the continuation specifies punishment for مَن ظَلَمَ, an individual wrongdoer category.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"17:15\",\"ar\":\"وَلَا تَزِرُ وَازِرَةٌۭ وِزْرَ أُخْرَىٰ\"},{\"ref\":\"18:86\",\"ar\":\"وَجَدَ عِندَهَا قَوْمًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"18:87\",\"ar\":\"أَمَّا مَن ظَلَمَ فَسَوْفَ نُعَذِّبُهُۥ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus moves from finding a people to the option of punishing them, and the next ayah identifies the wrongdoer as the punitive target.\",\"limits\":\"17:15 speaks about accountability before God; it does not directly state the rules governing Dhu al-Qarnayn's authority.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"X1\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"18:90 closely repeats the focus's journey frame for the same traveler, reversing مغرب/مطلع and تَغْرُبُ/تَطْلُعُ. At the western endpoint the sun sets in a spring and people are nearby; at the eastern endpoint it rises over a people with no screen from it. The paired scenes make the focus one pole of a deliberately contrasted solar journey.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"18:86\",\"ar\":\"حَتَّىٰٓ إِذَا بَلَغَ مَغْرِبَ ٱلشَّمْسِ وَجَدَهَا تَغْرُبُ فِى عَيْنٍ حَمِئَةٍۢ وَوَجَدَ عِندَهَا قَوْمًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"18:90\",\"ar\":\"حَتَّىٰٓ إِذَا بَلَغَ مَطْلِعَ ٱلشَّمْسِ وَجَدَهَا تَطْلُعُ عَلَىٰ قَوْمٍۢ لَّمْ نَجْعَل لَّهُم مِّن دُونِهَا سِتْرًۭا\"}],\"activation\":\"The same until-arrival and finding formula links the two solar endpoints and their peoples.\",\"limits\":\"The pairing establishes narrative and wording contrast, not a cosmological claim about the sun's physical route.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}"}}
